WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.726 --> 00:00:03.393
(intense music)

00:00:04.771 --> 00:00:06.789
- [Ram] Everyone says that I'm mad.

00:00:06.789 --> 00:00:09.406
And they ask me that question,
what draws me to the ice.

00:00:09.406 --> 00:00:13.352
And what amazes me is
how many people follow.

00:00:13.352 --> 00:00:15.215
- [Announcer] High in the
mountains of Southern Africa,

00:00:15.215 --> 00:00:17.007
there are 15 fearless swimmers competing

00:00:17.007 --> 00:00:20.733
in one of the most extreme races on Earth.

00:00:20.733 --> 00:00:24.440
With no wetsuits, no
protection, they must swim

00:00:24.440 --> 00:00:28.607
a single kilometer in water
cold enough to kill them.

00:00:29.760 --> 00:00:34.117
They call it a race, but
most would call it insanity.

00:00:34.117 --> 00:00:36.469
As founder Ram Barkai will tell you,

00:00:36.469 --> 00:00:39.517
the members of the International
Ice Swimming Association

00:00:39.517 --> 00:00:42.024
don't just battle to the finish,

00:00:42.024 --> 00:00:44.607
they're fighting to stay alive.

00:00:50.006 --> 00:00:52.673
(machine whirs)

00:00:54.636 --> 00:00:57.219
(gentle music)

00:01:02.519 --> 00:01:05.098
- Swimming cold water
has become my passion.

00:01:05.098 --> 00:01:07.237
Okay, we need another task
force for these ones, eh?

00:01:07.237 --> 00:01:10.485
The ice was the icing on the cake.

00:01:10.485 --> 00:01:12.068
I find ice extreme.

00:01:13.972 --> 00:01:17.612
I'm attracted to extreme challenges.

00:01:17.612 --> 00:01:20.913
No doubt, swimming in
ice is a dangerous sport.

00:01:20.913 --> 00:01:22.189
- Go Ram!
- Go Ram!

00:01:22.189 --> 00:01:23.563
- [Ram] It's not adrenaline junkie,

00:01:23.563 --> 00:01:25.340
polar bear type of experience.

00:01:25.340 --> 00:01:28.252
You actually have to swim one kilometer

00:01:28.252 --> 00:01:31.753
extremely fast in water under five degrees

00:01:31.753 --> 00:01:34.312
in just Speedo, goggles and a cap.

00:01:34.312 --> 00:01:36.453
These are the rules.

00:01:36.453 --> 00:01:39.867
I founded the International
Ice Swimming Association.

00:01:39.867 --> 00:01:41.991
I'd love it to become a proper

00:01:41.991 --> 00:01:45.463
internationally-recognized sport.

00:01:45.463 --> 00:01:47.266
- It's a double hit, physiologically,

00:01:47.266 --> 00:01:49.536
it’s an ice swim, which is challenging,

00:01:49.536 --> 00:01:50.753
and you're at altitude.

00:01:50.753 --> 00:01:54.816
Most of you have not
been at altitude before.

00:01:54.816 --> 00:01:57.586
- We were in Africa, in
the mountain of Lesotho

00:01:57.586 --> 00:02:00.554
and we found ice and
we're having the national

00:02:00.554 --> 00:02:03.832
South African ice championship.

00:02:03.832 --> 00:02:06.535
- Nowhere in Southern
Africa are you gonna find

00:02:06.535 --> 00:02:10.535
water five degrees in the
lakes, it's only here.

00:02:13.057 --> 00:02:14.849
- [Ram] Last year, we had about 13

00:02:14.849 --> 00:02:17.599
national championships like this.

00:02:18.810 --> 00:02:23.111
We have now swimmers in 28
countries around the world.

00:02:23.111 --> 00:02:25.194
- We're doing an ice swim in Africa,

00:02:25.194 --> 00:02:28.702
which is a bit ridiculous
if you ask anyone.

00:02:28.702 --> 00:02:31.207
We also think it's ridiculous.

00:02:31.207 --> 00:02:33.656
- It's a massive mental
challenge to get in that water

00:02:33.656 --> 00:02:36.415
and to perform physically for
an extended period of time.

00:02:36.415 --> 00:02:39.409
You keep going, takes a lot of mind power,

00:02:39.409 --> 00:02:40.933
and that's what I love, I love pushing

00:02:40.933 --> 00:02:43.850
the boundaries in these conditions.

00:02:52.233 --> 00:02:53.958
- [Ram] It's not a sport where you have to

00:02:53.958 --> 00:02:56.958
be lean, mean and beautiful to win.

00:02:58.718 --> 00:03:01.742
You have to have around 20% body fat.

00:03:01.742 --> 00:03:04.119
You have to be fit, you have to train

00:03:04.119 --> 00:03:08.286
so your body and your mind know
how to deal with the shock.

00:03:12.538 --> 00:03:15.121
Panic is the number one killer.

00:03:27.323 --> 00:03:28.708
- Initially, you can't tell if the water's

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:32.307
very hot or very cold, it almost burns.

00:03:32.307 --> 00:03:34.700
The first three minutes is painful

00:03:34.700 --> 00:03:38.867
and then you sort of settle
in and everything goes numb.

00:03:39.964 --> 00:03:41.938
- Their body temperature starts to drop.

00:03:41.938 --> 00:03:44.067
Getting into this water poses the hazard

00:03:44.067 --> 00:03:46.567
of hypothermia to the athlete.

00:03:49.021 --> 00:03:50.365
- [Ram] It causes havoc in your body,

00:03:50.365 --> 00:03:52.030
to your blood pressure,
to your heart rate,

00:03:52.030 --> 00:03:54.530
to your oxygen, to your brain.

00:03:55.965 --> 00:03:57.507
- [Woman] There's only one thing that goes

00:03:57.507 --> 00:03:59.757
through your mind: breathe.

00:04:00.609 --> 00:04:02.115
The ice takes your breath
away and if you don't

00:04:02.115 --> 00:04:06.147
control it, you can get
into a world of trouble.

00:04:06.147 --> 00:04:09.365
- [Ram] The average time in
the water is 15 to 20 minutes,

00:04:09.365 --> 00:04:11.213
which is still safe.

00:04:11.213 --> 00:04:14.140
It's a hugely mental sport.

00:04:14.140 --> 00:04:17.329
For me, it's mind over matter.

00:04:17.329 --> 00:04:19.531
- It's not you racing
against someone else,

00:04:19.531 --> 00:04:22.234
it's about you against
the ice and the cold.

00:04:22.234 --> 00:04:26.713
And basically competing
against your own mind.

00:04:26.713 --> 00:04:29.693
- Cold starts to affect the
function of the nervous system

00:04:29.693 --> 00:04:33.587
and so confusion, delirium
sometimes happens.

00:04:33.587 --> 00:04:35.293
The athletes can start to hallucinate

00:04:35.293 --> 00:04:36.902
or they can have amnesia.

00:04:36.902 --> 00:04:41.069
- If you feel dizzy and
disorientated, you should get out.

00:04:41.955 --> 00:04:43.566
It's such a hard decision to make,

00:04:43.566 --> 00:04:46.028
you're so close to finish.

00:04:46.028 --> 00:04:48.611
(gentle music)

00:04:54.486 --> 00:04:56.736
(cheering)

00:04:58.507 --> 00:05:00.355
- As you come out of the
water, the brain starts

00:05:00.355 --> 00:05:02.524
releasing blood from the core.

00:05:02.524 --> 00:05:06.134
That process is what
we call the after-drop.

00:05:06.134 --> 00:05:07.118
In ten minutes, suddenly your core

00:05:07.118 --> 00:05:10.310
body temperature can drop from 34 to 30.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:13.125
And that causes a havoc.

00:05:13.125 --> 00:05:17.292
If you haven't been there,
it's a scary experience.

00:05:18.632 --> 00:05:20.799
- There you go.
- Good job.

00:05:30.726 --> 00:05:33.933
- You're in the proper ICU,
doctors in recovery unit

00:05:33.933 --> 00:05:37.573
to make sure that no
one goes over the edge.

00:05:37.573 --> 00:05:40.526
It's literally like going
up the roller coaster,

00:05:40.526 --> 00:05:43.772
you know that feeling,
slowly, slowly, slowly,

00:05:43.772 --> 00:05:46.000
and suddenly you get there.

00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:50.397
When you get there, you
see people's eyes go like.

00:05:50.397 --> 00:05:52.609
We call it the devil's look.

00:05:52.609 --> 00:05:54.821
They know everything what's
going on, they hear everything.

00:05:54.821 --> 00:05:56.611
They just can't respond.

00:05:56.611 --> 00:06:00.528
You are in like a massive
sort of tunnel vision

00:06:01.500 --> 00:06:03.833
focused on hanging in there.

00:06:09.618 --> 00:06:11.662
- I feel the life going back into me.

00:06:11.662 --> 00:06:16.046
I don't know if that's
a good thing though.

00:06:16.046 --> 00:06:17.165
- I'm starting to turn the corner,

00:06:17.165 --> 00:06:19.811
so I'm feeling better about
life, now I wanna live.

00:06:19.811 --> 00:06:21.889
(laughs)

00:06:21.889 --> 00:06:26.056
- After the dust has settled,
you feel like a champion.

00:06:27.474 --> 00:06:30.386
- Before they start swimming,
everyone ask himself,

00:06:30.386 --> 00:06:31.816
"Why am I doing this?

00:06:31.816 --> 00:06:33.942
"What am I trying to prove to myself?"

00:06:33.942 --> 00:06:36.027
Guys, well done to all of you.

00:06:36.027 --> 00:06:39.459
I know it wasn't easy and I
know that not everyone finished.

00:06:39.459 --> 00:06:41.726
I think it's part of the process.

00:06:41.726 --> 00:06:44.178
(cheering)

00:06:44.178 --> 00:06:48.345
Then after I finish, I can't
wait for the next adventure.

00:06:50.282 --> 00:06:52.115
And I'm high for days.

00:06:54.904 --> 00:06:58.135
My big dream is to get it
to the Winter Olympic games

00:06:58.135 --> 00:07:00.560
as a sport, it'll be my legacy.

00:07:00.560 --> 00:07:02.154
The beauty of what we're doing here today

00:07:02.154 --> 00:07:06.237
is to show that if there's
a will, there's a way.

